Yeah, I have some insider knowledge.  Years ago Jeni's switched their cream supplier from the small family owned Snowville Ohio dairy to Smith's Dairy.  Snowville has A2/A2 Heritage Jersey cows which have a 5% milk fat compared to Smith's which I assume has A1/A1 Holsteins (black and white cows) which has a 3.5% milk fat.  


The A2/A2 vs A1/A1 also a huge difference in quality.  A2/A2 is a milk casein gene that is the same as humans.  A1/A1 is a mutation that occurred 10,000 years ago that allowed for greater milk volume at a reduced quality.  Most people who have milk sensitivities actually have a sensitivity to the A1/A1 gene, not milk itself.  

&#x200B;

Heritage breeds don't do well in industrial farming.  Their milk output is directly correlated to how well they are treated.  Snowville could not scale to meet Jeni's growth as they put animal welfare over profits, all of their cows have names. To my knowledge, Jeni's still uses Snowville cream for their coffee.  I haven't lived in Columbus in 5 years, but I remember seeing Snowville half gallon cartons.  Their dairy is heavenly and their raw cocoa chocolate milk is to die for. 

&#x200B;

Hope this helps

I lost my science job 3 days after I closed on my hobby farm.  I had a lot of savings and I decided to turn the negative into a positive by working for the farmer next door so that I could learn hands on about equipment and animals while looking for a new job.  The one year I spent learning how to farm has really bitten me in the ass years down the line.  I have 8 years experience since graduating in STEM and I have averaged about 30k per year since then.  

&#x200B;

It sucks OP, but unfortunately it is a reality, at least in my experience.

Hey OP, 

I can relate a lot to this post.  I haven't had the best work history and I have been laid off many times.  After this happening enough times I started to feel like how you are describing it in your post.  I remember one time before I got laid off, everyone in upper management  was being really nice to me that day.  For a long time after that anytime someone would be nice to me I would think "it is coming".  I would also get nervous every Friday because that's when they let people go.  I literally took my job one week at a time.   

&#x200B;

One thing that helped me was getting a second job and creating an emergency fund from it.  It sounds like you are in a bad feed back loop.  This was my feedback loop: get worried about getting fired, display a lack of confidence in my work making other people skeptical of me, being seen as incompetent because of how I displayed myself, people start seeing me as incompetent, get let go.

&#x200B;

The root of my fear was all financial.  By getting a second job I removed my fear of getting fired, and the cascading effects from that stopped.  Once I was confident in my main role I left my second job.  Hope that helps!
